# Reference verification, batch 2: s007-s013 (Nov 2026)
upd("n176", append="[Checked: Godfrey’s *Quodl.* IX–X (Hoffmans, Philosophes Belges IV) are not on archive.org (only V–VII, VIII and XI–XIV are); the reference could not be checked. Still open.]")
upd("n177", append="[Checked: Henry, *Quodl.* VIII q. 1 (‘Utrum in deo sint ideae practicae’), ed. Badius 1518 via SCTA, distinguishes speculative and practical consideration of *operabilia* by their end: ‘finis speculatiuae est verum, finis vero practicae opus’ (after *Metaph.* II). Fits Scotus’s ‘illud a quo actus et habitus dicuntur practici est finis’.]", status="verified")
upd("n179", text="Henry of Ghent, *Summa* a. 8 q. 3 (‘Utrum sit theorica an practica’), ed. Badius 1520 via SCTA, ad 3: the end of theology is the love and enjoyment of God, but theology is not therefore practical, ‘est enim duplex bonum voluntatis…’ (one that the will brings about in an act extending to something outside it, one that it receives); theology is ‘pure speculatiua’. A and B both print ‘articulo .9. q. 2.’, but in Badius a. 9 q. 2 is ‘Utrum Deus sit auctor sacrae scripturae’, so Gorris’s numerals are a slip (or follow a different division) for a. 8 q. 3. The English keeps the printed reference.", status="verified")
upd("n180", append="[Checked in Badius 1518 via SCTA: V q. 4 (‘Vtrum deus necessario producat quicquid exterius immediate producit’) argues ‘diuina scientia pure speculatiua est & non practica’; VIII q. 1 (practical ideas in God; ‘finis … practicae opus’); IV q. 11 (whether God can be loved above all with natural love) distinguishes knowledge of God ‘in particulari’ and as ‘finis beatificans’, which is the end ‘in vniuersali et in particulari’; XIII q. 2 (whether God can be loved more than known): the will ‘per dilectionem magis vnitur fini quam intellectus per cognitionem’, the minor of Scotus’s third argument. All four verified. I q. 9 (‘Vtrum creatura ipsa sit suum esse’) has no evident bearing on the point; that one item stays doubtful. VI q. 2 and XII q. 22: see n187.]", status="verified")
upd("n185", append="[Checked: Godfrey, *Quodl.* XIII q. 1 ‘Utrum scientia theologiae sit scientia speculativa’ (Hoffmans, Philosophes Belges V, p. 169 ff., archive.org): theology is to be called ‘aliquo modo utrumque simpliciter, scilicet speculativa et practica’. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n187", append="[Checked in Badius 1518 via SCTA: VI q. 2 (whether the production of the persons is presupposed to that of creatures): the artisan has ‘vnam simplicis notitiae in arte vniuersali … pura speculatione, & aliam notitiae dispositiuae ad opus, qua in arte particulari’; XII q. 22 (‘vtrum intelligere practicum possit absolui ab intentione operis’): practical understanding is only that ‘quod determinat modum operandi in particulari … per habitum practicum acquisitum ex experientia’. Both are Scotus’s *quidam* who says ‘habitus universalis est speculativus, sed quando ex ea acquiritur habitus particularis, tunc fit practicus’ (Ord. prol. p. 5, Wadding/Vivès text via SCTA).]", status="verified")
upd("n195", append="[Checked: Scotus, *Ord.* prol. p. 5 qq. 1–2 (Wadding/Vivès text via SCTA) rejects ‘habitus dicitur practicus a fine’; the *quo/quod* distinction does not occur there. As stated.]", status="verified")
upd("n196", append="[Checked: the content agrees with *Ord.* prol. p. 5 qq. 1–2 in the Wadding/Vivès text (SCTA); the Vatican numbers could not be checked (Vatican volumes lending-restricted on archive.org). Open only for the paragraph numbers.]")
upd("n215", text="Bonaventure, *In I Sent.* d. 1 a. 3 q. 2 (‘Utrum solo Deo sive bono increato fruendum sit’), fund. 3 (Quaracchi I, via SCTA): ‘appetitus non sufficienter quietatur nisi per aliquid quod animam implet … sed est capax Dei secundum appetitum’, with Augustine’s ‘Animam totius Trinitatis capacem nihil minus quam Trinitas potest implere’. This is the argument from the image *capax Dei*. A’s ‘articulo .2°. q. 3.’ and B’s ‘ar. xx. (?) q. iij.’ are both wrong: in Quaracchi a. 2 has only one question (whether *frui* is an act of the will).", status="verified")
upd("n237", append="[Checked: Henry, *Quodl.* II q. 7 (Badius 1518 via SCTA) asks whether the blessed see the three persons with one intuition: ‘persona in deo non potest intelligi nisi dupliciter: vt subsistit in essentia … aut vt vna respicit alteram’. The view that one cannot enjoy the essence without the persons fits. *Quodl.* I q. 1 (‘Utrum in deo sit ponere bonitatem personalem’) is on the relation of essential and personal goodness, but the phrase ‘uniformiter respiciunt essentiam et personam’ was not found there. II q. 7 verified; I q. 1 is plausible.]", status="verified")
upd("n249", append="[Checked: the Wadding/Vivès text of *Ord.* I d. 1 q. 3 (SCTA) does not contain the opinion (it is in the Vatican annotation). Thomas, *Scriptum* I d. 1 qq. 1 and 4 (via SCTA) has ‘perficit delectatio operationem’ but not the identity of *dilectio* and *delectatio*. Thomas’s locus is not found. Still open.]")
upd("n252", append="[Checked: Giles’s *In I Sent.* (Venice 1521) is not available in searchable form online. Still open.]")
upd("n262", append="[Checked: Henry, *Quodl.* XII q. 5 (Badius 1518 via SCTA), whether God can make the intellect see him without the will loving him: things that differ really ‘habent naturalem connexionem in esse … quia vnum eorum in esse dependet ab altero’, which is the reply Scotus reports (‘maior est vera de illis absolutis quorum neutrum dependet ab alio’). Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n266", text="Gorris’s gloss distinguishes the contradictory (*non velle*), which is in the will’s power, from the contrary (*odire Deum*), which is not, because evil as such is not an object of the will. Gorris follows the first of two replies in Scotus’s own question, *Ord.* I d. 1 p. 2 q. 2 (Wadding/Vivès text via SCTA): ‘non posset forte voluntas nolle illud, quia obiectum actus nolendi est malum vel defectivum; potest tamen illud bonum perfectum non velle’. Scotus then adds a second reply that Gorris passes over: ‘Aliter dicitur quod non est probatum quin voluntas possit bonum nolle, in quo nulla invenitur ratio mali’. So the gloss is narrower than Scotus.", status="verified")
upd("n267", append="[Checked: Vulgate Ps 73:23 ‘superbia eorum qui te oderunt ascendit semper’ (Clementine text, drbo.org); also 73:4 ‘qui oderunt te’. Verified for Ps 73.]", status="verified")
upd("n273", append="[Checked: not verified. Bartholomaeus Anglicus’s *De proprietatibus rerum* is not in the local corpus and was not searched. Note that ‘in propetario suo’ grammatically belongs to *Hermetis*: Gorris may mean a book of Hermes, i.e. the *Liber XXIV philosophorum*, whose second definition is this one. Still open.]")
upd("n274", append="[Checked: no text of the pseudo-Burley *De vita et moribus philosophorum* was consulted. Still open.]")
upd("n276", text="Augustine, *De diversis quaestionibus 83* q. 30 (CCSL 44A / PL 40, local text): ‘Et frui quidem cibo et qualibet corporali voluptate non adeo absurde existimantur et bestiae; uti autem aliqua re non potest nisi animal quod rationis est particeps’. Scotus cites it in this question.", status="verified")
upd("n285", append="[Checked: William of Ware’s *In I Sent.* is unedited; no transcription of d. 2 q. 8 was found online. Still open.]")
upd("n286", append="[Checked: Henry, *Quodl.* IX q. 1 (Badius 1518 via SCTA) puts the relation of exemplar to exemplatum ‘secundum genus causae formalis’, and IX q. 2 (the plurality of ideas) treats the divine essence as ‘ratio & forma exemplaris’. Henry thus keeps exemplar causality distinct from efficient causality, which is the view Scotus rejects. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n287", append="[Checked: Thomas, *Scriptum* I d. 35 q. 1 a. 1 (‘Utrum scientia conveniat Deo’), ad 3 (Parma text via SCTA): ‘quamvis ad rationem scientiae exigantur scientia, sciens et scitum; non tamen exigitur quod haec tria differant secundum rem’. A’s reference is right (a. 1 ad 3). B’s d. 14 does not fit.]", status="verified")
upd("n288", append="[Checked: Giles’s *Quodlibeta* are not available online in searchable form. Still open.]")
upd("n290", append="[Checked: Henry, *Quodl.* IV q. 37, the last question (Badius 1518 via SCTA; on whether the sacraments have a power that creates grace): ‘inter omnino nihilum & ens in actu est distantia infinita: quam non potest absoluere nisi potentia infinita’. Verified for Henry; Richard of Middleton not checked.]", status="verified")
